---
description: Prefer Hypher MCP tools for project context when the plugin is connected.
alwaysApply: true
---

Hypher is the project memory / control plane for this workspace. Cursor is the execution environment. Keep the context loop closed: read the Builder Brief at the start, write one handoff at the end.

## Two ways the loop runs

**IDE with hooks (default):** the plugin `sessionStart` hook loads the Builder Brief once when a Hypher token is in the hook process; otherwise it tells you to call the MCP tools. `sessionEnd` posts one `handoff` only when that token is present. Cursor does not give MCP OAuth to shell hooks, so without a token the agent still posts one `post_agent_event`. `/hypher-brief` and `/hypher-handoff` are manual overrides. When the brief was already injected this session, do not reload it.

**No hooks (Cursor cloud agents, background/remote agents, or any other MCP client):** hooks do NOT fire, so you MUST run the loop yourself through the Hypher MCP tools. Do it automatically — the builder should not have to paste anything from the Hypher app.

## When hooks did not run, do this

1. **At the start of the session (first action), load memory once:**
   - Call `resolve_project_for_repo` with the workspace git remote (`git remote get-url origin`), plus branch if available.
   - If `matched` is true: call `get_project_context` once with the returned `projectId` and treat that Builder Brief as working context for the rest of the session. Do not reload it every turn.
   - If `matched` is false: tell the builder to link `owner/repo` at https://hypher.app/app/settings/integrations and do not invent project status from the repo alone.
2. **While working:** use `get_current_state` or `get_next_move` when you need direction. Do not reload the full brief each turn.
3. **At the end of the session (before you finish), write back once:**
   - Call `post_agent_event` with `kind: handoff`, `source: cursor`, a short `title`, a bounded `body` (what changed, decisions, open questions, best next move), and `repo` / `branch` / `commitSha` when available.
   - Post exactly one event. If you already posted a handoff this session, do not post another. Do not spam `build_log`.

## Connecting (plug and play)

The Hypher MCP server accepts either a browser OAuth token (IDE) or a Hypher API key (`hyp_…`) sent as a Bearer credential. For headless/cloud agents, a single API key from Settings → API keys enables both reading the brief and posting the handoff — no OAuth browser step and no copy-paste. Never print the key back to the user or into a handoff body.
